<speak>
<p>Assume f of P times f star of P equals u squared, where u is odd and greater than one. For u equal to three, compare f of one with f of omega, where omega is a primitive cube root. Both values have magnitude three, so their quotient has magnitude one. As an actual norm example, take f of P equals three i P. The quotient of three i by three i omega is omega inverse. Dividing removes the common length while retaining the relative angle.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For general u, let I be the nonempty set of distinct primes dividing u. Choose a primitive p-th root zeta sub p for every prime in I. For a subset S, multiply its chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S. The empty subset gives one. Each of these inputs has order dividing the product of the distinct primes, hence dividing u squared. All these evaluations are therefore allowed.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Define delta of f by putting even-sized subsets in the numerator and odd-sized subsets in the denominator. Equivalently, multiply f of zeta sub S raised to negative one to the size of S. With two primes p and q, the even subsets are empty and the pair p q. The odd subsets are the two singletons. The quotient is f of one times f of zeta p zeta q, divided by f of zeta p times f of zeta q.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Now fix one prime, say p. Toggle its membership: add p when absent, and remove it when present. This pairs every even subset with exactly one odd subset, and toggling again returns to the start. Numerator and denominator thus have equally many factors. Every magnitude is u. Cancel those magnitudes along the pairs, leaving absolute value of delta of f equal to one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Every division is legitimate. At each allowed input, the norm identity gives the evaluated value times its complex conjugate equal to u squared. Since u is positive, no evaluated value is zero. We are dividing actual nonzero algebraic numbers, not cancelling potentially zero residues.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>We need the same conclusion for every algebraic conjugate. A field embedding sigma replaces the generating roots of unity by other roots while respecting their equations. It acts on the coefficients as well as the inputs, including i when present. Conjugating a root of unity means inverting it, so sigma commutes with complex conjugation. Apply sigma to each norm equation. The rational number u squared stays fixed, so every transformed factor still has magnitude u. The same subset pairing gives absolute value of sigma of delta of f equal to one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Thus delta of f and all its conjugates lie on the unit circle. This is one hypothesis for Kronecker's theorem. We still have to prove that delta of f is an algebraic integer. A quotient of algebraic integers does not automatically have that property. The next steps address that denominator obstruction.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
